     ccllii..lluuaa — bootloader command line interpreter module

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ccllii..lluuaa contains the main functionality required to add new CLI commands,
     which can be executed at the loader prompt.

     Before hooking into the functionality provided by ccllii..lluuaa, it must be in‐
     cluded with a statement such as the following:

           local cli = require("cli")

   AAddddiinngg nneeww ccoommmmaannddss
     New loader commands may be created by adding functions to the object re‐
     turned by requiring the ccllii..lluuaa module.

     For instance:

           local cli = require("cli")

           cli.foo = function(...)
                   -- Expand args to command name and the rest of argv.  These arguments
                   -- are pushed directly to the stack by loader, then handed off to
                   -- cli_execute.  cli_execute then passes them on to the invoked
                   -- function, where they appear as varargs that must be peeled apart into
                   -- their respective components.
                   local _, argv = cli.arguments(...)

                   print("This is the foo command!")
                   for k, v in ipairs(argv) do
                           print("arg #" .. tostring(k) .. ": '" .. v .. "'")
                   end
                   -- Perform a loader command directly.  This will not get dispatched back
                   -- to Lua, so it is acceptable to have a function of the exact same name
                   -- in loader.  Lua will have the first chance to handle any commands
                   -- executed at the loader prompt.
                   loader.perform("foo")
           end

     This function may be invoked by a user at the loader prompt by simply
     typing ffoooo.  Arguments may be passed to it as usual, space-delimited.

   DDeeffaauulltt CCoommmmaannddss
     The ccllii..lluuaa module provides the following default commands:

     ••   aauuttoobboooott

     ••   bboooott

     ••   bboooott--ccoonnff

     ••   rreellooaadd--ccoonnff

     ••   ddiissaabbllee--ddeevviiccee

     ••   ddiissaabbllee--mmoodduullee

     ••   eennaabbllee--mmoodduullee

     ••   ttooggggllee--mmoodduullee

     ••   sshhooww--mmoodduullee--ooppttiioonnss

     For aauuttoobboooott, bboooott, and bboooott--ccoonnff, the core.lua(8) module will load all
     ELF modules as-needed before executing the equivalent built-in loader
     commands.  All non-kernel arguments to these commands are passed in the
     same order to the loader command.

     The rreellooaadd--ccoonnff command will reload the configuration from disk.  This is
     useful if you have manually changed currdev and would like to easily
     reload the configuration from the new device.

     The eennaabbllee--mmoodduullee, ddiissaabbllee--mmoodduullee, and ttooggggllee--mmoodduullee commands manipulate
     the list of modules to be loaded along with the kernel.  Modules black‐
     listed are considered disabled by ttooggggllee--mmoodduullee.  These commands will
     override any such restriction as needed.  The sshhooww--mmoodduullee--ooppttiioonnss command
     will dump the list of modules that loader has been made aware of and any
     applicable options using paged output.

     The ddiissaabbllee--ddeevviiccee command sets the environment variable that disables
     the device argument.

